{
  "id": "wheels",
  "name": "Wheels, Tires & Hubs",
  "summary": "Gen 6 Camaro corners use bolt-on sealed hub bearings (front non-driven, rear splined with an axle nut), 5x120 mm M14x1.5 studs, and 60° conical lug nuts at 190 N·m (140 ft-lb). The ZL1 1LE runs forged 19x11 ET≈11 / 19x12 ET≈41 wheels on Goodyear Eagle F1 SuperCar 3R 305/30R19 and 325/30R19; race builds use lightweight forged 18–19 in wheels on R-compounds or slicks, with extended studs, open-end nuts and TPMS deleted or replaced by motorsport TPMS.",
  "overview": "Stack-up (outboard): knuckle → hub bearing (bolted from inboard) → wheel studs pressed in the hub flange → rotor hat (or one-piece rotor) → optional hub-centric spacer → wheel (hub-centric on 66.9 mm pilot) → lug nuts. Rear adds the halfshaft CV stub through the splined hub, clamped by the axle nut.\n\nRace considerations: wheel diameter is dictated by the brake package (factory 390 mm rotors need 19 in; Essex/AP 372 mm kits clear 18 in ATS-V-size wheels). Offsets near OE (front ET≈11 on 11 in, rear ET≈41 on 12 in) keep scrub radius and fender clearance sane; ZL1 1LE owners commonly run ≈ -3.2° front camber. Many rulebooks set a maximum wheel width/tire size per class and require full thread engagement of lug nuts (≥ 1x stud diameter) – fit extended studs whenever spacers or thick race-wheel pads are used.\n\nHub bearings are consumables on a slick-shod 3,700 lb car: check play every event and replace on a schedule; worn bearings cause pad knock-back and long pedal. Re-torque lug nuts after the first session on freshly mounted wheels and never use an impact gun for final torque.",
  "buildNotes": [
    "Phase 4 (after suspension): install front/rear hub bearings with new bolts (front 100 N·m first pass + GM final angle; rear 130 N·m), wheel speed sensors, then brakes.",
    "Press extended studs (if used) with hubs on the bench before installation.",
    "Phase 6: insert halfshafts and torque new rear axle nuts (270 N·m) with the car on the ground or rotor locked.",
    "Template-check race wheels against the chosen calipers before ordering; specify offset to match OE track unless suspension is re-engineered.",
    "Mount/balance tires; install metal valve stems or motorsport TPMS; record sensor IDs.",
    "Phase 11: torque lug nuts 190 N·m (140 ft-lb) in star pattern, re-check after first shakedown session; set cold pressures from hot targets."
  ],
